  "worked_solution": "We model the number of unprocessed records using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nR(t)={R}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.25t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${R}_{0}$ is the initial number of unprocessed records.\n\nWe're told that there are $20\\,000$ unprocessed records at the start of the scan, so we have ${R}_{0}=20\\,000.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of unprocessed records has decreased to $1\\,000.$ That is,\n\n$$\nR({t}_{1})=1\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,000 & =20\\,000{e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{20\\,000} & ={e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.05 & ={e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.05) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.05) & =-0.25{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.05)}{-0.25} \\\\ {t}_{1} & \u224811.982\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $12$ minutes for the number of unprocessed records to decrease to $1\\,000.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
